{
  "passage": "The Constitution of India gives every citizen certain basic rights, called Fundamental Rights. They protect people against the misuse of power by the government and by other people. The Right to Equality says that all persons are equal before the law, and that no one can be discriminated against on the basis of religion, caste, sex or place of birth. Everyone has equal access to public places such as parks, wells and hotels. The Right to Freedom includes freedom of speech and expression, the freedom to form associations, the freedom to move to and live in any part of the country, and the freedom to practise any profession. The Right against Exploitation forbids human trafficking, forced labour and the employment of children below 14 years in factories and mines. The Right to Freedom of Religion allows everyone to follow the religion of their choice. Cultural and Educational Rights allow religious and linguistic minorities to set up their own schools to preserve their culture. The Right to Constitutional Remedies allows a citizen to go directly to the courts if any of these rights is violated. Dr B. R. Ambedkar, who led the drafting of the Constitution, called this right the heart and soul of the Constitution, because without it all the other rights would have no value.",
